Reading /bin/form back into C
=============================

form.c was reconstructed from the binary, which we have no source for.
This records how, because the same is to be done to every Micronix
binary in that position, and most of what follows is about Whitesmiths
C rather than about form.


What the image looks like
-------------------------

    disas filesystem/bin/form

    whitesmiths type 94 symlen 9 norelocs
    symbols: 0 text: 100(341b) data: 4000(c6d) bss: 0

16 byte header, then text at 0x100, then data at 0x4000.  To get at the
data segment in the file, seek to 16 + textlen.

STRIPPED.  "symbols: 0" is what makes this work harder than it needs to
be, and it defeated one of disas's own features - see below.


Whitesmiths calling conventions, learned here
---------------------------------------------

Every function opens by CALLing a fixed helper rather than opening code
for its own frame:

    H34c0:  ld hl,0 / add hl,sp / ex de,hl / ex (sp),hl / jp (hl)

which leaves the frame pointer in DE, and closes with JP to

    H34c7:  ex de,hl / ld sp,hl / pop de / ...

So a function body reads as "CALL <framehelper>, LD HL,-N, ADD HL,SP,
LD SP,HL" - and N is the frame size.  Locals are addressed off DE as
"LD HL,FFF9H / ADD HL,DE".  Arguments are at positive offsets from DE:
"LD HL,0004H / ADD HL,DE" is the first one.

A switch is a third helper of the same family:

    H349b:  ld a,(hl) / inc hl / or (hl) / jp nz,cmp
            inc hl / ld a,(hl) / inc hl / ld h,(hl) / ld l,a / jp (hl)
            ld a,c / inc hl / cp (hl) ...

reached as "LD BC,<value> / LD HL,<table> / JP <helper>".  The table is
4-byte entries of [address, value], ending at an entry whose address is
zero - and THAT entry's second word is the default target.

disas already knew how to walk this, but only when a symbol named
"c.switch" pointed at the helper.  form has no symbols, so all sixteen
of its request handlers disassembled as instructions.  disas now finds
the helper by its byte signature (see src/tools/disas.c); do that first
on any stripped image or the jump tables will read as nonsense that
happens to decode.


How form itself is put together
-------------------------------

Main loop, at H0327: read a line into the buffer at 0x4404, and

    if (buf[0] == '.')  docommand(buf);     /* H03f9 */
    else                dotext(buf);        /* H0edd */

which is the whole of "if the first character of a line is a period,
form attempts to interpret the line as a command".

docommand looks the name up in a table of {name, code} pairs at 0x4a38,
walked with strcmp (H2fce), and switches on the code.  The names live
in the text segment at 0x03c9 as sixteen 3-byte entries.  Do not assume
the switch values are indices into the name storage - they are not; read
the pointer table.  The mapping is:

    bp  1  H0506      ls  8  H047c
    br  2  H0476      nf  9  H0469
    ce  3  H067c      pl 10  H054e
    fi  4  H0459      rm 11  H061e
    fo  5  H04bd      sp 12  H04d1
    he  6  H04a9      ti 13  H064c
    in  7  H05b3      ul 14  H06ac
    ta 16  H05ed      bd 15  H06d9

    default   H0466   (which is just the function epilogue)

That mapping is what makes the small handlers make sense, and it is how
the variables below were identified:

    H117d       break - flush the line being filled.  .br is nothing
                but a call to it, and every request that changes the
                shape of the page calls it first.
    0x4000      fill, a 16 bit flag.  .fi is "call break, fill = 1"
                and .nf is "call break, fill = 0".  Nothing else.
    0x4404      the input line buffer.
    0x4a1a      read by .ti.

form has no messages of its own.  The only strings in the image are
libc's errno table at 0x2cdc, so a large part of that 13K is library,
not program.  Its only flags are -t and -o (at 0x0113 and 0x0116), and
the fopen modes r/a/w are at 0x1976.


What is traced and what is not
------------------------------

Traced out of the binary: the main loop and its dot test, the request
set and their dispatch codes, which handlers are trivial, the fill flag,
that .br is the break routine, the two command line flags.

Taken from man1/form.1 rather than the binary: the defaults (fill on,
indent 0, right margin 60, page length 66, .bd/.ce/.ul default 1), and
the meaning of each request.

NOT yet verified against the binary: pagination arithmetic - where the
header sits, how many blank lines top and bottom, exactly when a page
breaks.  form.c currently puts three blank lines, the header, and a
blank at the top, and the footer three from the bottom, which produces
a 67 line page where it should produce 66.  The handlers at H0506 (.bp)
and H04a9/H04bd (.he/.fo) are where the real answer is.

THE TEST THAT SETTLES IT is to run the original under the simulator and
diff its output against ours over the whole man tree:

    for f in filesystem/usr/man/man*/*; do
        <run /bin/form under the simulator on $f>  > a
        ./form.host $f                             > b
        cmp a b
    done

183 pages is a large and free corpus of real input.  Do that before
trusting any of this.
